    "result_text": "The only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, and the suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `timezone` field derived from the owner's region via the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\nFollowing the existing \"single source of truth\" pattern for locality rules, I made three changes:\n\n1. **`OwnerLocality.java`** \u2014 added the pinned `REGION_TIMEZONE` map (`NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney`, `VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne`, `QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane`) and a `timezoneForRegion(region)` lookup, alongside the existing region/postcode tables so the rule can't drift.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"timezone\", expression = \"java(timezone(owner))\")` and a `timezone(owner)` default that feeds the owner's already-derived `locality` (region) into `OwnerLocality.timezoneForRegion(...)`. This guarantees `timezone` stays consistent with `locality` (both flow from the `customerCode` region), returning `null` for `UNKNOWN` so the field is simply absent then.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `timezone` string property to the owner schema (the DTO is generated from this spec), documenting the mapping.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` (Sydney/postcode 2000 \u2192 `locality: NSW`, `timezone: Australia/Sydney`) passes.",
